  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "rlNJAcsywkr0YuqzpWWQeJDntx4=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:33.235Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Appian is recorded as not a complement. It is proprietary low-code process automation that occupies the same slot as Camunda and the application layer as well, so the conclusion is displacement, not addition.\n\nThis is worth stating separately from the Mendix and OutSystems calls because the reasoning is doubled. Against a pure low-code app platform the collision is at the app layer; against a pure BPMN engine there is a documented seam where the record layer attaches. Appian closes both, which removes the fallback argument that works for Camunda partners.\n\nTwo leads carry this finding, both from the provisional v2 ranking tail: NextWave Consulting in the UK at \"2 \u00b7 decide thesis first\" and Valcon in NL at \"9 \u00b7 do not pitch\". Both source rows record long-standing Appian practices, and Valcon's also records roughly ten years of Mendix work \u2014 so this lead sits inside two displacement groups at once, which is consistent with its do-not-pitch marking.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (2)\n\n**2 \u00b7 decide thesis first**\n- NextWave Consulting (UK)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Valcon (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> NOT a complement \u2014 Appian is proprietary low-code process automation and occupies the same slot as Camunda AND the app layer. Displacement, not addition.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:31:33.235403Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:33.235408Z"
    }
  },
